  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜として (assuming; as)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Add として (toshite) after a noun to mean 'as (a role/capacity)' or to set up a hypothetical frame. It establishes the perspective or role through which something is considered.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses として to frame scenarios hypothetically — 'assuming this is the case' or 'as if this were so'.\n\nMore examples:\n• 先生 (sensei, 'teacher') → 先生として (sensei toshite, 'as a teacher')\n• 友達 (tomodachi, 'friend') → 友達として (tomodachi toshite, 'as a friend')\n• 日本人 (nihonjin, 'Japanese person') → 日本人として (nihonjin toshite, 'as a Japanese person')\n\nNote: として can also mean 'even (one)' in negative sentences: 一人として来なかった (hitori toshite konakatta, 'not even one person came')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "先生 (sensei, 'teacher') + として (toshite, 'as') → 先生として (sensei toshite, 'as a teacher')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜みたいだ (it seems like; it's like)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Add みたいだ (mitai da) after a noun, verb, or adjective to express resemblance or conjecture — 'it seems like X' or 'it's like X'. This is the casual equivalent of ようだ.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses みたいだ to compare feelings and situations to something familiar, creating vivid imagery.\n\nMore examples:\n• 夢 (yume, 'dream') → 夢みたいだ (yume mitai da, 'it's like a dream')\n• 死んだ (shinda, 'died') → 死んだみたいだ (shinda mitai da, 'it seems like it died')\n• 春 (haru, 'spring') → 春みたいだ (haru mitai da, 'it's like spring')\n\nNote: Before a noun, use みたいな: 夢みたいな世界 (yume mitai na sekai, 'a world like a dream'). Before a verb, use みたいに: 鳥みたいに飛ぶ (tori mitai ni tobu, 'fly like a bird')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "夢 (yume, 'dream') + みたいだ (mitai da, 'seems like') → 夢みたいだ (yume mitai da, 'it's like a dream')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜出す (to start doing; to burst out)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Attach 出す (dasu, 'to put out') to the verb stem (masu-stem) to express a sudden or explosive beginning of an action — 'burst out doing' or 'start to do'.\n\nExample from this song: 抜け出す (nukedasu) = 'to break out / escape' — from 抜ける (nukeru, 'to slip through') → 抜け (nuke, stem) + 出す (dasu, 'to burst out'). Also: 逃げ出す (nigedasu) = 'to run away' — from 逃げる (nigeru, 'to flee') → 逃げ (nige, stem) + 出す (dasu)\n\nMore examples:\n• 走る (hashiru, 'to run') → 走り出す (hashiridasu, 'to start running suddenly')\n• 泣く (naku, 'to cry') → 泣き出す (nakidasu, 'to burst into tears')\n• 笑う (warau, 'to laugh') → 笑い出す (waraidasu, 'to burst out laughing')\n\nNote: 〜出す emphasizes the sudden, often unstoppable nature of the action starting. Compare with 〜始める (hajimeru), which is a neutral 'begin to'."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "抜ける (nukeru, 'to slip through') → 抜け (nuke, stem) + 出す (dasu, 'burst out') → 抜け出す (nukedasu, 'to break out') / 逃げる (nigeru, 'to flee') → 逃げ出す (nigedasu, 'to run away')"
    },
    {
      "name": "命令形 (imperative form)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Change the verb ending to create a direct command. For -ru verbs (ichidan), replace る with ろ. For -u verbs (godan), change the final syllable to its え-row equivalent.\n\nExample from this song: 覚ませ (samase) = 'Wake up!' — from 覚ます (samasu, 'to wake/awaken') → change す (su) to せ (se)\n\nMore examples:\n• 走る (hashiru, 'to run') → 走れ (hashire, 'Run!')\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べろ (tabero, 'Eat!')\n• 立つ (tatsu, 'to stand') → 立て (tate, 'Stand!')\n\nNote: For godan verbs: す→せ, く→け, む→め, つ→て, ぬ→ね, ぶ→べ, う→え, る→れ. For ichidan verbs: る→ろ. This is very direct — use with caution in real life, but it's extremely common in songs and anime."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "覚ます (samasu, 'to awaken') → 覚ませ (samase, 'Wake up!')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜のか (rhetorical question)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Add のか (no ka) at the end of a sentence to create an emotionally charged question, often rhetorical. の adds an explanatory tone, and か marks the question. Together they express doubt, frustration, or deep wondering.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses のか to question reality with emotional weight — 'Is it really...?' or 'Can it be that...?'\n\nMore examples:\n• 何 (nani, 'what') → 何なのか (nan na no ka, 'what is it, really?')\n• できる (dekiru, 'can do') → できるのか (dekiru no ka, 'can it really be done?')\n• 本当 (hontō, 'true') → 本当なのか (hontō na no ka, 'is it really true?')\n\nNote: In casual male speech, のか often drops to のか without rising intonation, making it sound reflective rather than questioning. It adds more emotional weight than plain か alone."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "できる (dekiru, 'can do') + のか (no ka, rhetorical question) → できるのか (dekiru no ka, 'can it really be done?')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜に変わる (to change into)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Use noun + に (ni, 'into') + 変わる (kawaru, 'to change') to express transformation from one thing to another. The particle に marks what something changes into.\n\nExample from this song: 声が耳鳴りに変わった (koe ga miminari ni kawatta) = 'the voice changed into a ringing in the ears' — from 声 (koe, 'voice') + が (ga, subject) + 耳鳴り (miminari, 'ringing') + に (ni, 'into') + 変わった (kawatta, 'changed')\n\nMore examples:\n• 雨が雪に変わる (ame ga yuki ni kawaru, 'the rain changes to snow')\n• 緑が赤に変わる (midori ga aka ni kawaru, 'green changes to red')\n• 笑顔が涙に変わった (egao ga namida ni kawatta, 'the smile turned to tears')\n\nNote: 変わる (kawaru) is intransitive (changes by itself). 変える (kaeru) is transitive (someone changes it). 色を変える (iro wo kaeru, 'to change the color')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "声 (koe, 'voice') + が (ga) + 耳鳴り (miminari, 'ringing') + に (ni, 'into') + 変わった (kawatta, 'changed') → 声が耳鳴りに変わった (koe ga miminari ni kawatta, 'the voice changed into ringing')"
    }
  ]
